    description: 'Russian byliny epic-narrative tradition: oral-historical-narrative poetry transmitted by skomorokhi (medieval) and later Old-Believer skaziteli into the 19th-and-20th-century. Subjects: Kievan-Rus heroes (bogatyri), Novgorod merchants, religious-historical events. Performance: solo-male recitation accompanied by gusli (Slavic psaltery, 5-to-25 strings). Recitative melody constrained to 3-to-5 notes per byliny; verses chained without strict-metric repetition. Documented through 19th-century Onega-region collecting expeditions (Hilferding, Rybnikov). Distinguished from Russian-folk-orchestra tradition by solo-narrative-not-ensemble form and by archaic-not-codified repertoire.',
    exemplars: ['Olga Sergeyeva — Onega-region byliny field recordings', 'Trofim Ryabinin lineage recordings (RGAFD archives)', 'Anglo-Russian collaborative recordings — Holy Russia: Music of the Northern Folk', 'Nikolai Krivopolenov — Pinega-region recordings'],
