    description: 'Hungarian-Romani urban-restaurant ensemble tradition: magyar nóta (popular-art song, 19th-century onward) and verbunkos (recruitment-dance, late-18th-century military-recruitment origin). Standard prímás-led ensemble: lead violin (prímás), second violin (kontrás), viola (kontra), cimbalom (hammered dulcimer), double bass. Cimbalom developed by József Schunda (1874 Budapest) into pedaled chromatic concert version. Distinguished from neighboring Eastern-European Jewish ensemble tradition by Hungarian-language vocal repertoire and by verbunkos rhythmic-foundation distinct from freygish-mode dance lineage.',
    exemplars: ['Sándor Lakatos — prímás recital recordings', 'Lajos Boross — Budapest restaurant ensemble recordings', 'Roby Lakatos — modern crossover recordings', 'Kalman Balogh — solo cimbalom recitals'],
