    description: 'The Persian lālā\'ī (لالایی, cradle-song) tradition continuous from medieval Persian domestic-vocal practice through pre-revolutionary modern-Iranian domestic singing and post-1979 diaspora practice; defined by Persian dastgāh-derived modal-melodic-vocabulary (Shur, Dashti, and Abu Ata gushehs frequent in lālā\'ī practice), microtonal koron-and-sori pitch inflection characteristic of Persian classical-and-popular music, free-meter or slow rocking phrasing, melismatic ornament drawing on tahrir vocal-trill technique, Farsi-Persian text register with the refrain "lā lā lā lā" giving the form its name, intimate maternal-singing performance context. Distinguished from Arabic tahwidah by Persian dastgāh-not-maqam modal framework and by Persian-Farsi linguistic register.',
    exemplars: ['Traditional "Lā lā lā lā gol-e laleh" (Lullaby of the tulip)', '"Lā lā lā gol-e parpar"', 'Hayedeh — modern Iranian cradle-song recordings', 'Hossein Alizadeh / Hosein Behrouzinia — classical-Persian cradle-song settings', 'Mohammad-Reza Shajarian — classical Persian vocal cradle adjacent'],
