    description: 'The Hindi-Urdu lori (लोरी, cradle-song) tradition continuous from North-Indian medieval domestic practice through Bollywood-filmi-cinema appropriation from the 1940s onward into modern South-Asian domestic singing across India, Pakistan, Bangladesh, and the broader South-Asian diaspora; defined by raga-melodic-vocabulary inflecting the lullaby frame (Yaman, Bhairavi, and other tender-affect ragas common in lori practice), free-meter alap-like opening with rocking metric body, characteristic Hindustani gamaka ornament-vocabulary applied gently, occasional harmonium drone accompaniment in domestic context, Hindi-Urdu literary-text register, intimate maternal-and-grandmother singing performance context. Bollywood-filmi lori (Mughal-e-Azam "Mohe Panghat Pe," Albela "Bholi Surat Dil Ke," etc.) extended the form into commercial cinema and globalized it.',
    exemplars: ['"Aaja ri aa nindiya" (canonical traditional)', '"Chanda hai tu mera suraj hai tu" (Aradhana 1969 film-lori)', '"So jā re lādle so jā" (traditional)', '"Mai ri main kaa se kahūn" (Dastak 1970 film-lori)', '"Nindiya ke pār laga de naiyā" (traditional)'],
    crossRefs: ['lullaby.middleEastern', 'functionalSong.southAsianPop', 'improvOnFrame.indianClassicalImprov'],
