    description: 'The Tamil thālāttu (தாலாட்டு, cradle-song) tradition continuous from medieval Tamil-Nadu domestic-vocal practice through Sangam-era literary-cradle tradition (the form is documented in Tamil literature back to the early-medieval period) into modern Tamil-language domestic singing across Tamil-Nadu, Sri Lanka, Malaysia, Singapore, and the broader Tamil diaspora; defined by Carnatic-raga melodic-vocabulary inflecting the lullaby frame (Neelambari is the canonical Carnatic lullaby-raga, with Anandabhairavi and other tender-affect ragas also common), characteristic Carnatic gamaka ornament-vocabulary, ⁶⁄₈ rocking or free-meter phrasing, Tamil literary-text register with frequent classical-Tamil and Sangam-poetry resonance, intimate maternal-and-grandmother singing context. "Kanmaniyē kandidu nilavē" is the canonical refrain syllable-pattern.',
    exemplars: ['"Kanmaniyē kandidu nilavē" (canonical traditional)', '"Aararō ārirarō" (Sangam-era traditional)', 'M. S. Subbulakshmi — traditional thālāttu recordings', '"Malarē mauname"-cradle-adjacent', 'Bharathiyar cradle-poetry settings'],
