    description: 'The Korean jajanga (자장가, cradle-song) tradition continuous from Joseon-era Korean domestic-vocal practice through 20th-century colonial-era and post-Korean-War modern-Korean domestic singing across South Korea, North Korea, and the Korean diaspora; defined by Korean minsogak-folk-modal vocabulary (the gyemyeon-jo / pyeong-jo system), characteristic syllable "jajang jajang" (자장자장) functioning as melodic refrain and tradition-name, slow ⁶⁄₈ or free-meter rocking phrasing, gentle ornament without the elaborate melisma of pansori practice, Korean-Hangul literary-text register, intimate maternal-and-grandmother singing context. The Korean cradle-song tradition is one of the strongest documented in modern-Korean domestic culture with the jajanga remaining a recognized cultural-categorical form into present-day practice.',
    exemplars: ['"Jajang jajang uri agi" 자장자장 우리 아기 (canonical traditional)', '"Nae sarang nae aga" 내 사랑 내 아가 (traditional)', '"Saebyeok Byeol" 새벽별 (cradle-adjacent)', 'Yang Hee-eun — traditional cradle-song recordings', 'Yoon Bok-hee — folk-cradle-revival recordings'],
