    description: 'The Māori waiata tangi (mourning-song) tradition continuous from pre-contact Māori ritual-practice through 19th-20th century continuous performance at tangihanga (Māori funeral rites) into modern Māori-cultural-revival; the waiata tangi is one of the codified Māori waiata categories alongside waiata aroha (love-song) and waiata oriori (lullaby) within the broader Māori sung-poetic tradition. Defined by narrow-range pentatonic-leaning melodic-vocabulary characteristic of Māori traditional waiata practice (te reo Māori voice-production), free-meter recitation with rhythmic-text-pulse, Māori-language te reo text register with extensive whakapapa (genealogy) naming and deceased-praise content, unaccompanied solo-or-group vocal performance, ritual-bounded performance context at the marae during tangihanga. The waiata tangi is integral to the structure of tangihanga and is performed by family-and-tribal members at specific stages of the funeral process.',
    exemplars: ['Hirini Melbourne — traditional waiata tangi recordings 1980s-1990s', 'Mahinarangi Tocker — waiata tangi compositions', 'Whirimako Black — modern waiata tangi-revival', 'Documented Te Awa Kairangi waiata tangi tradition', 'Tūhoe tribal waiata tangi corpus (Best 1925 ethnography)'],
