    description: 'The Zulu izibongo praise-poetry tradition continuous from pre-colonial Zulu royal-court praise-singer (imbongi) practice through 19th-century Shaka-kaSenzangakhona-era continuous performance into modern South-African Zulu cultural-practice; the imbongi is the hereditary praise-poetry-and-political-orator role with specialized voice-projection-and-recitation training. Defined by unaccompanied heightened-speech declamation (the imbongi practice is closer to oratorical-declaimed-speech than to song proper), free-meter rapid-paced recitation, Zulu tonal-language vocal-melody constrained by speech-tone patterns, characteristic vocal-projection-and-overtone-rich timbre, isiZulu-language text register with specialized izibongo metaphorical-and-praise vocabulary, accumulated-epithet structural form (each Zulu king is praised through a documented sequence of epithets across generations). The izibongo tradition extended into modern South-African political-oratory — Nelson Mandela was eulogized through imbongi praise-poetry at his funeral.',
