    description: 'The Shona mbira-and-vocal praise-music tradition continuous from pre-colonial Zimbabwe Karanga-Shona ritual-practice through colonial-period continuous performance into modern Zimbabwean Shona-cultural practice; the mbira dzavadzimu ("mbira of the ancestors") is the canonical Shona ritual-and-praise instrument used at bira ceremonies for ancestor-praise-and-spirit-possession-induction. Defined by interlocking-mbira-pattern foundation (the kushaura "leading" and kutsinhira "interweaving" parts woven by two mbira players), seven-tone Shona modal vocabulary (the mbira tuning system is distinct from twelve-tet Western), call-and-response vocal-foreground with kudeketera-poetic-vocal-improvisation over the cycling mbira pattern, ChiShona text register with specialized ancestor-praise vocabulary, cyclical-form structure permitting hours-long bira ceremony performance.',
