    description: 'The Hawaiian praise-and-name-chant tradition continuous from pre-contact Hawaiian ritual-practice through 19th-century Hawaiian-Kingdom-era continuous performance and publication (mele inoa, like kanikau funeral-chants, were published in Hawaiian-language newspapers from the 1830s onward) into 20th-century Hawaiian-cultural-revival; the mele inoa ("name-song") is the canonical Hawaiian praise-chant-form naming the praise-receiver (typically a chief, ali\'i royal-figure, or beloved person) through genealogical-and-place-name lineage. Defined by oli (chant) free-meter recitation in characteristic Hawaiian voice-production (the kāmākau voice-production with specific glottal-onset on \'okina), Hawaiian-language text register with extensive praise-of-the-recipient and place-naming, unaccompanied solo-vocal performance, ritual-bounded performance context. Distinguished from kanikau (funeral-chant) by praise-of-living-person function rather than mourning-of-the-deceased.',
