    description: 'The Mande-and-Wolof West-African praise-song tradition continuous from medieval Mali Empire era (13th-15th century Sundiata Keita era when the jeli/griot caste-tradition was codified) through colonial-period continuous performance into modern Mande-and-Wolof and Mande-diaspora practice across Mali, Senegal, Guinea, Gambia, Burkina Faso, and the broader Sahel; the jeli (Mande) / géwél (Wolof) / griot (French-colonial-loanword) is the hereditary praise-singer-and-historian role with documented professional-genealogical lineage (the Kouyaté and Diabaté and Tounkara families being among the most-documented jeli lineages). Defined by kora (21-string harp-lute) and ngoni and balafon and voice arrangement, Mande-pentatonic modal vocabulary, characteristic praise-narrative-form structure (the canonical Sundiata epic is the foundational jeli-praise-narrative), Manding-Maninka-Bambara-Wolof-Mandinka text register with specialized praise-and-genealogical vocabulary, accumulated-name-and-epithet declamatory style. Distinguished from the broader West-African pop traditions descended from jeli practice (Salif Keita and Toumani Diabaté\'s Symmetric Orchestra crossing into popular form).',
