    description: 'The Russian harvest-song (жнитвенные песни zhnitvenye pesni) tradition continuous from medieval-Russian-agricultural village-labor practice through 19th-century Russian-folklorist documentation through Soviet-era Pyatnitsky and Voronezh-Russian-Folk-Choir canonical performance (the Soviet-era Russian-folk-choir movement institutionalized and partly-staged the Russian-harvest-song corpus) into modern Russian-folk-revival continuous practice. Foundational repertoire integrates rye-and-wheat-harvest songs accompanying scything-and-binding labor (the Russian three-field-rotation agricultural-system meant harvest-songs were performed across distinct seasonal-task contexts). Defined by unaccompanied women\'s-group-vocal arrangement, Russian-language text register, characteristic Russian-folk modal vocabulary, characteristic group-women-labor seasonal-context.',
    exemplars: ['Pyatnitsky Russian Folk Choir — canonical Soviet-era Russian-folk', 'Voronezh-Russian-Folk-Choir canonical Soviet-era', 'Russian-village zhnitvenye-pesni continuous-living tradition', 'Lyudmila Zykina — canonical 20th-century Russian-folk-vocal', '"Расти расти, лен" canonical Russian-folk-harvest'],
    crossRefs: ['fieldWork.eastAsian', 'wedding.european', 'balladPoetry.slavicBard'],
