    axes: { harm: -1, pitch: 0, ornament: 1, meter: 0, density: -1, transmission: -2, improv: 0, soundTech: -2, intensity: 0, voice: 2, timbre: 0, percussion: -1, cyclicity: 1 },
    description: 'The Mande-and-Wolof West-African harvest-and-field song tradition continuous from premodern Mande-and-Wolof agricultural village-labor practice through colonial-period continuous performance into modern West-African-and-West-African-diaspora practice across Mali, Senegal, Guinea, Gambia, Burkina Faso, and the broader Sahel; foundational repertoire integrates rice-planting and millet-harvesting and rice-pounding songs accompanying specific agricultural-tasks. The Mande-Wolof harvest-song tradition is distinct from the broader Mande-Wolof praise-song-jeli tradition by labor-coordination-task primary-function rather than praise-of-named-individuals. Defined by call-and-response group-vocal foreground with kora-and-balafon optional-accompaniment, Manding-Maninka-Wolof text register, Mande-pentatonic modal vocabulary, characteristic agricultural-labor task-coordination structure.',
    exemplars: ['Mande-Wolof village rice-planting and millet-harvest continuous-living tradition', 'Toumani Diabaté — Mali field-song integrated kora canonical', 'Salif Keita — Mali field-and-labor song lineage canonical', 'Mande-Wolof rice-pounding women\'s-song traditional canonical', 'Hugh Tracey — early-20th-century West-African field-recordings canonical'],
