    description: 'The Andean Quechua-and-Aymara huayno-de-trabajo ("huayno-of-work") tradition continuous from pre-Hispanic Andean-agricultural-labor practice (the Andean ayni reciprocal-labor exchange tradition has been documented as integrating extensive song-coordination across collective-labor tasks including potato-and-quinoa planting, maize-and-quinoa harvest, llama-and-alpaca shearing, irrigation-canal maintenance — minka collective-labor mobilization) through Spanish-colonial-era continuous performance into modern Quechua-and-Aymara-speaking Andean-rural practice across Bolivia, Peru, and Ecuador. Defined by quena-and-siku-and-voice arrangement, Quechua-Aymara-and-Spanish text register, huayno-2-4 rhythmic-and-pentatonic-modal vocabulary, characteristic collective-labor task-coordination structure.',
    exemplars: ['Modern Quechua-Aymara Andean rural-village huayno-de-trabajo continuous-tradition', 'Inti-Illimani — nueva-canción canonical (broader Andean-folk lineage)', 'Ricardo Pita — Bolivian-Aymara huayno canonical', 'Andean ayni collective-labor song-coordination canonical', 'Folkloric Andean-village potato-planting song traditional'],
